          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "How many arrangements are there of the letters of ABCDEFG so that A and B are not adjacent, and C and D are not adjacent? (Letters from different pairs may be adjacent.)",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p7-q9-proposal",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Jane proposes: first arrange EFG in ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "3!",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " ways. Then, choose gaps for A and B in ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "4\\times3",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " ways, then choose gaps for C and D in ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "6\\times5",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " ways. Is this correct?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No; $2640$ arrangements.\n\nJane misses arrangements such as EACBFDG, where C later separates A and B in the same original gap. Subtract each forbidden adjacent-pair count and add back their overlap.",
      "worked": "No. For example, EACBFDG satisfies both restrictions, but A and B occupy the same original E–F gap, separated by C. Jane's method never produces it.\n\nThere are $7!$ unrestricted arrangements. Treating A and B as one block gives $2!\\times6!$ arrangements; the same count applies to C and D. Arrangements with both pairs adjacent are subtracted twice, so add back $2!\\times2!\\times5!$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=7!-2!\\times6!-2!\\times6!+2!\\times2!\\times5!\\\\N&=5040-1440-1440+480\\\\N&=2640\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
